 Run tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or newer, with native TypeScript support).
 
+`createPatch(from, to)` creates an [RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902.html)
+JSON Patch such that `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` equals `to`.
+Both inputs must be JSON values, using the same validation rules as `applyPatch`.
+Neither input is mutated, and patch values are independent copies. Equal values
+produce `[]`, ignoring object key order. Objects are compared recursively; arrays
+are aligned by equal elements so insertions and removals preserve unchanged
+elements, and changed ranges are compared recursively. Operations use `add`,
+`remove`, and `replace`; a change of value type can replace that value, including
+at the root. The patch is not guaranteed to have the minimum possible byte size
+or operation count.
+
+```ts
+createPatch({ settings: { theme: 'dark', keep: true } }, {
+  settings: { theme: 'light', keep: true },
+}); // [{ op: 'replace', path: '/settings/theme', value: 'light' }]
+```
+
 `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies JSON Merge Patch as defined by
 [RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386.html), returning an independent
 JSON value without mutating either input. Object patches merge members recursively;
